# UI Component Conventions

This guide outlines the conventions for creating and maintaining UI components in our codebase.

## Component Structure

- **Use Function Components**
  - All components should be defined as function components
  - Use arrow function syntax for consistency
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use arrow function syntax
  const MyComponent = () => {
    return (
      <div>Content</div>
    );
  };

  // ❌ DON'T: Use function declaration syntax
  function MyComponent() {
    return (
      <div>Content</div>
    );
  }
  ```

- **Default Export Pattern**
  - All components should be exported as default exports
  - Export statement should be placed at the end of the file
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Export at the bottom of file
  const MyComponent = () => {
    // Component implementation
  };

  export default MyComponent;
  ```

## Client vs Server Components

- **Client Components**
  - Add `"use client";` directive at the top of the file when using hooks or browser APIs
  - All components with event handlers must be client components
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Mark interactive components as client components
  "use client";

  import { useState } from "react";

  const InteractiveComponent = () => {
    const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
    // Component implementation
  };
  ```

- **Server Components**
  - When a component doesn't use hooks, event handlers, or browser APIs, leave out the "use client" directive
  - Keep data fetching logic in server components when possible

## State Management

- **Use React Hooks**
  - Prefer `useState` for local component state
  - Use `useEffect` for side effects and lifecycle handling
  - Consider custom hooks for shared logic
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use hooks for state management
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    // Effect code
  }, [dependencies]);
  ```

## Props and Parameters

- **Destructure Props**
  - Destructure props in the function parameters
  - Provide default values when appropriate
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Destructure props with defaults
  const ButtonGradient = ({ title = "Gradient Button", onClick = () => {} }) => {
    return (
      <button className="btn btn-gradient animate-shimmer" onClick={onClick}>
        {title}
      </button>
    );
  };
  ```

- **Prop Documentation**
  - Include JSDoc-style comments for components with complex props
  - Document each prop's purpose, especially for reusable components
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Document component props with comments
  // ButtonCheckout handles payment processing via Stripe
  // - priceId: The Stripe price ID to charge
  // - mode: 'payment' (one-time) or 'subscription' (recurring)
  const ButtonCheckout = ({ priceId, mode = "payment" }) => {
    // Component implementation
  };
  ```

## Styling

- **Use Tailwind Classes**
  - Use Tailwind CSS classes for styling components
  - Group related classes together for readability
  - Use conditional classes with template literals or ternary operators
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use Tailwind with conditional styling
  <div className={`relative flex ${isOpen ? "gap-4" : "gap-2"}`}>

  // ✅ DO: Group related classes together
  <div className="flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-3 p-2">
  ```

- **Component-Specific Utility Classes**
  - Define specialized utility classes in `app/globals.css` for reused styling patterns
  - For example, `.btn-gradient` for gradient buttons

## Image Handling

- **Use Next.js Image Component**
  - Use `next/image` for all images for optimization
  - Always specify width and height props
  - Add appropriate alt text for accessibility
  - Use `priority` prop for critical above-the-fold images
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use Next.js Image component with required props
  <Image
    src={imageSrc}
    alt="Descriptive alt text"
    width={500}
    height={300}
    className="object-cover rounded-lg"
    priority={isPriority}
  />
  ```

## Component Organization

- **Component Sections**
  - Structure larger components into logical sections using comments
  - For complex components, consider splitting into subcomponents
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use sections for complex components
  // Header section
  const Header = () => {
    // Header implementation
  };

  // Main component 
  const PageComponent = () => {
    return (
      <>
        <Header />
        {/* Content section */}
        <main>...</main>
      </>
    );
  };
  ```

- **File Naming**
  - Use PascalCase for component file names (e.g., `ButtonGradient.js`)
  - Name should clearly indicate the component's purpose

## Interactivity

- **Event Handlers**
  - Prefix event handler functions with `handle` (e.g., `handleClick`, `handleSubmit`)
  - Define handlers within component functions, not outside
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use handle prefix for event handlers
  const handleClick = () => {
    // Handle click logic
  };
  
  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>;
  ```

- **Loading States**
  - Implement loading states for async operations
  - Use loading indicators to provide user feedback
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Handle loading states
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);
  
  const handleSubmit = async () => {
    setIsLoading(true);
    try {
      // Async operation
    } finally {
      setIsLoading(false);
    }
  };
  
  return (
    <button disabled={isLoading}>
      {isLoading ? <span className="loading loading-spinner loading-xs"></span> : "Submit"}
    </button>
  );
  ```

## Accessibility

- **Semantic HTML**
  - Use semantic HTML elements (`button`, `nav`, `header`, etc.)
  - Don't use divs for interactive elements
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use semantic elements
  <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>
  
  // ❌ DON'T: Use non-semantic elements for interaction
  <div onClick={handleClick}>Click me</div>
  ```

- **ARIA Attributes**
  - Add appropriate ARIA attributes when needed
  - Ensure proper keyboard navigation
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use ARIA attributes when appropriate
  <button
    aria-expanded={isOpen}
    aria-controls="menu-content"
    onClick={toggleMenu}
  >
    Menu
  </button>
  ```

## Error Handling

- **Error States**
  - Handle potential errors gracefully
  - Provide fallbacks for failed operations
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Handle errors gracefully
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  
  try {
    // Operation that might fail
  } catch (e) {
    setError(e.message);
    console.error(e);
  }
  
  if (error) {
    return <div className="text-error">Error: {error}</div>;
  }
  ```

## Performance

- **Memoization**
  - Use `useMemo` for expensive calculations
  - Consider `React.memo` for components that render often but rarely change
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Memoize expensive calculations
  const expensiveResult = useMemo(() => {
    return computeExpensiveValue(a, b);
  }, [a, b]);
  ```

- **Dynamic Imports**
  - Use dynamic imports for large components not needed immediately
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use dynamic imports for large components
  const HeavyComponent = dynamic(() => import('./HeavyComponent'), {
    loading: () => <p>Loading...</p>
  });
  ```

## Configuration Integration

- **Use Config Values**
  - Import app-wide configuration from `@/config` when needed
  - Don't hardcode values that might change between environments
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use config values instead of hardcoding
  import config from "@/config";
  
  // Use config.appName, config.auth.callbackUrl, etc.
  ```

## Responsive Design

- **Mobile-First Approach**
  - Design for mobile first, then add Tailwind breakpoint classes for larger screens
  - Use responsive utility classes (`md:`, `lg:`, etc.)
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Use responsive classes
  <div className="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-4 md:gap-8">
  
  // ✅ DO: Use responsive text sizes
  <h1 className="text-2xl md:text-4xl lg:text-6xl">Heading</h1>
  ```

## SEO and Metadata

- **Use Metadata API**
  - Define page metadata using Next.js's metadata API in page components
  - Include title, description, and Open Graph data
  ```javascript
  // ✅ DO: Define metadata for pages
  export async function generateMetadata({ params }) {
    return {
      title: "Page Title",
      description: "Page description",
      openGraph: {
        // OG data
      }
    };
  }
  ```

These conventions ensure consistency, maintainability, and best practices across our UI components.
